generation alongside provenance cues to reduce hallucination risks while prioritizing process quality over direct    
answer generation [^10.3390_asi9010017].                                                                             
+                                                                                                                    
+## Semantic Governance and Ethical Implications                                                                     
+Emerging research critically examines the architectural limitations of source-grounding in RAG systems. Empirical   
tests across multiple RAG implementations reveal that mere source access does not prevent semantic governance        
failures or hallucinations under strict semantic dominance, indicating a need for advanced governance primitives     
[^10.5281_zenodo.18039273]. Furthermore, privacy benchmarks indicate that standard RAG assistants can inadvertently  
leak sensitive user data in over 26% of interactions, underscoring the necessity for privacy-by-design structural    
safeguards in personalized AI deployments [^10.48550_arxiv.2512.24848].                                              
+                                                                                                                    
+## Advanced RAG Architectures                                                                                       
+Beyond standard QA, RAG principles are being adapted for complex agentic workflows. The R-Debater framework         
introduces argumentative memory and retrieval grounding to generate coherent, stance-aligned multi-turn debates,     
demonstrating that combining retrieval with structured planning yields more faithful and logically consistent outputs
[^https:__openalex.org_W7118051612].                                                                                 
                                                                                                                     

